\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\title{Inexact Gradient Descent: Recovering Convergence}\author{EAR Authored Reference Walkthrough}\date{}\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}If the error bound tends to zero, the stable convolution tends to zero. Exact convergence follows, although a linear rate need not.\end{abstract}
\section{Setting and theorem}Let $Q$ be symmetric positive definite, with $\mu I\preceq Q\preceq LI$. Choose $0<\eta<2/L$ and $q=\max\{|1-\eta\mu|,|1-\eta L|\}<1$. For $x_{k+1}=(I-\eta Q)x_k-\eta e_k$ and $\|e_k\|\le\epsilon_k$,
\[\|x_k\|\le q^k\|x_0\|+\eta\sum_{s=0}^{k-1}q^{k-1-s}\epsilon_s.\]
\section{Proof}Diagonalize Q in an orthonormal eigenbasis. The spectral norm of I-eta*Q is at most q. Applying the triangle inequality gives ||x[k+1]|| <= q||x[k]|| + eta*epsilon[k]. Induction unrolls this recurrence into the stated bound. For any delta > 0, choose N such that epsilon[s] <= delta for s >= N. The finite prefix of the convolution vanishes as k grows; the remaining tail is at most eta*delta/(1-q). Taking a limit superior and then delta to zero proves the claim.
\section{Corollary}If epsilon[s] tends to zero, ||x[k]|| tends to zero. This claim concerns positive-definite quadratics and does not assert a linear rate for arbitrary decaying errors.
\section{Boundary case}In one dimension, take Q=mu, x[0]=0, 0<eta<=1/mu and e[k]=epsilon/(k+1). The contributions to x[k] have one sign, so |x[k]| >= eta*epsilon/k. This lower bound excludes a geometric rate.
\section{Numerical check}We use $Q=\mathrm{diag}(1,10)$, $\eta=0.1$, $x_0=(1,1)$ and $e_k=(0.10/(k+1),0)$. 81 saved states obey the bound. This numerical check is not a proof.
\section{Provenance and limitations}An authored reference task, not an EAR model episode or a new research result. Prepared review feedback led to explicit scope and the complete boundary argument. No conference acceptance or formal verification is claimed.
